#{[17592186164416 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164419] [17592186166533 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166551 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$"] [17592186166552 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."] [17592186166705 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f1/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"] [17592186167191 :question/worked-solution "Follow the arrows from the marked point. The trajectory initially moves upward and to the right. It reaches the steeper dashed nullcline with a vertical tangent, turns left, and then crosses the other dashed nullcline with a horizontal tangent. Afterward it bends downward and left. The matching trajectory is\n\n![](images/67/67273ae9c0f9d50f1a95da3c61039fa9cf262a7b34b93cc9e6f1fe26cd463a5f.png)"] [17592186167194 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186167194 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167216 :question/worked-solution "On the $x$-nullcline, $x^{\\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-3x.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, $y^{\\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac12x.\n$$"] [17592186167223 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 6\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1,2}=[\\pm 2\\text{i},3]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}(6t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}(6t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}(6t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}(6t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167223 :question/worked-solution "Set $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0$ in the general solution. This gives\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\sin(6t),\\qquad y(t)=3\\cos(6t).\n$$\nThe frequency is $6$ in both coordinates: $x'=-12\\cos(6t)=-4y$ and $y'=-18\\sin(6t)=9x$. Thus these two existing choices satisfy the system. The original source's $2t$ in the second coordinate is a typographical error."] [17592186167224 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=3x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=2\\pm 3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167224 :question/worked-solution "Set $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0$:\n$$\nx(t)=e^{2t}\\cos(3t),\\qquad y(t)=e^{2t}\\sin(3t).\n$$\nDifferentiating gives $x'=2x-3y$ and $y'=3x+2y$, so this pair satisfies the system."] [17592186167230 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186167230 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"] [17592186167242 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$"] [17592186167243 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186167243 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"] [17592186167994 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y) = \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x(t),y(t))\\end{bmatrix}$ is a vector-valued function of $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $y(t).$\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t,$ the solution has a position\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′} &=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors. \n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2).$ Then, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-4x+2y \\\\  & =-4(1)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x-5y \\\\  & =(1)-5(-2) \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } \\\\ {y}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ 11\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y &={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)"] [17592186171015 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186171019] [17592186171225 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186171226] [17592186171471 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186171473] [17592186172565 :question/worked-solution "Substitute each marked point into $y′=2-xy$. The slopes at $(-1,2)$, $(1,3)$, and $(0,-2)$ are respectively $4,-1,2$. Select the diagram with a steep positive segment on the left, a negative segment at $(1,3)$, and a positive segment at $(0,-2)$."] [17592186172600 :question/worked-solution "Substitute each marked point into $y′=x^2-2y$. The slopes at $(1,2)$, $(-1,-1)$, and $(2,-2)$ are respectively $-3,3,8$. Select the diagram with those signed segment slopes."] [17592186172641 :question/worked-solution "Compare segments across each horizontal row. In the selected diagram every segment in a row has the same slope, so the slope depends only on $y$. It therefore cannot represent the stated dependence on both $x$ and $y$."] [17592186174388 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186174390] [17592186174934 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186174938] [17592186174940 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186174941] [17592186174946 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186174951]}
